    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3615?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Teaching) - Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (Teaching) - Psychology | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor (Teaching) - Psychology Detroit, MI, United States | req3615 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/11/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor (Teaching) - Psychology Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Assistant Professor (Teaching) - Psychology at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): The Department of Psychology invites applications for a full-time (9 months) Assistant Professor (Teaching) position (non-tenure-track). We seek an active and engaged colleague with demonstrated professional and teaching interests within Psychology. Preferred candidates should be able to teach several of our undergraduate core major courses, which include Introduction to Psychology, Statistics, Research Methods, Learning and Memory, Perception, Cognition, Developmental Psychology, and Health Psychology. Course format is primarily in-person, but may also include hybrid, or online delivery. Teaching load is 3:3 (fall and winter semesters) Candidates are also expected to participate in department committees and activities. The successful candidate will join a vibrant and engaged academic unit. Our faculty have achieved national and international reputations for excellence in their respective fields of research. We offer a collegial environment, state-of-the art facilities, excellent programming, and extensive training opportunities for teaching-related careers, such as those offered by the University’s Office of Teaching and Learning. Teach in-person, hybrid and/or online courses in the Psychology undergraduate program. Courses could include Introduction to Psychology, Statistics, Research Methods, Learning and Memory, Perception, Developmental Psychology, Cognitive Psychology or Health Psychology. Assist program faculty and staff with student recruitment and retention initiatives, program assessment and other duties required to meet organizational needs. Serve on departmental committees. Qualifications: Candidates with a Ph.D. in Psychology or a related field are preferred. However, those who are ABD or hold a Master's degree and have considerable professional experience may be considered. Evidence of effective university teaching experience, including in person instruction using best practices in course design. Candidates should apply at jobs.wayne.edu. Include a cover letter that addresses: (1) teaching experiences and interests; (2) teaching philosophy, particularly regarding undergraduate instruction; and (3) commitment and readiness to work with a diverse population of students. Applicants should also provide a curriculum vitae (CV) with the names, titles, and contact information for three professional references, and a teaching portfolio that includes course evaluations and example syllabi. Questions about this search may be directed to Emily Grekin, Search Committee Chair, at grekine@wayne.edu. School/College/Division: H12 - College of Liberal Arts & Science Primary department: H1224 - Psychology Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Academic/Faculty Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Working conditions: This position must be filled pursuant to the provisions of a collective bargaining agreement, and as such may be filled by a qualified bargaining unit member, should one apply. Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3611?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "University Counselor III - Medical Academic & Student Programs",
      "description": "University Counselor III - Medical Academic & Student Programs | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) University Counselor III - Medical Academic & Student Programs Detroit, MI, United States | req3611 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/11/2026 Back to Search University Counselor III - Medical Academic & Student Programs Wayne State University is searching for an experienced University Counselor III - Medical Academic & Student Programs at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): The University Counselor III position will be responsible for providing academic and personal counseling services for approximately 200 medical students from matriculation through graduation.  This position reports to the Associate Dean of Student Affairs and Career Development. Responsible for the daily provision of academic and personal counseling services for a minimum case assignment of 200 medical students from matriculation through graduation. Work with students individually and in groups in various activities and programs focused on emotional health, academic success, professional development and career advising on a daily basis. Assist in the development, innovation, implementation and evaluation of health and wellness programming which meet the changing needs of medical students in a rapidly developing curriculum. Represent the Office of Student Affairs and Career Development on various School of Medicine and University committees and functions. Participate in the regional and national activities of the AAMC. Unique duties: Qualifications: Minimum Master’s degree required in counseling and guidance, student personnel, social work and/or psychology. Doctorate Degree preferred. Preferred qualifications: Five to Eight years of experience in working with students.  Medical student experience preferred. \n\nAbility to accommodate a high volume of students while individually addressing a wide variety of challenging academic and personal situations. \n\nExcellent problem-solving skills required.  \n\nCommitment to learning the organizational and curricular structure of the comprehensive undergraduate medical education program. \n\nProvide direct support to the developing leadership initiatives in Student Affairs ( career Advising, Health and Wellness, and Mentoring) \n\nDevelop and Implement programming to address the Health and Wellness of the student body. \n\nAdaptable to rapid organizational and curricular change. \n\nAbility to handle conflict resolution and crisis, emotional and psychological needs of the young adult medical student. \n\nKnowledgeable about mental health, human services referral agencies in the tri-county area. \n\nPossess outstanding written and verbal communication skills. \n\nResponsive and available to the needs of others. \n\nWork cooperatively and collaboratively in a team approach. \n\nMust be flexible, creative and have the ability to remain objective. \n\nDedicated to organizational excellence. \n\nGeneral knowledge and understanding of student policies and procedures and administrative structure. \n\nResearch skills/past track record of scholarly activity highly desirable School/College/Division: H06 - School of Medicine Primary department: H0671 Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Academic/Faculty Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Hourly rate: Salary minimum: Salary hire maximum: $115,950.00 Working conditions: Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3608?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Marketing & Communications Specialist, Partnership and Workforce",
      "description": "Marketing & Communications Specialist, Partnership and Workforce | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Marketing & Communications Specialist, Partnership and Workforce Detroit, MI, United States | req3608 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/10/2026 Back to Search Marketing & Communications Specialist, Partnership and Workforce Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Marketing & Communications Specialist, Partnership and Workforce at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): Job Purpose Provide comprehensive marketing and communications support for the Office of Partnerships & Workforce (P&W) to enhance the visibility, engagement, and impact of its offices, programs, services, and university-wide initiatives. Develop and implement marketing and communications activities that promote student success, career-engaged learning, workforce partnerships, international recruitment, global engagement, undergraduate research, executive and professional education, and other strategic priorities of the division. Create content for print, web, email, social media, and other communications channels; support branding, digital communications, and outreach efforts; and collaborate with university leadership and campus partners to ensure consistent messaging across multiple audiences. Reports to the Vice Provost or designee. Essential Functions (Essential functions are the primary duties/major job responsibilities that an employee must be able to perform, with or without reasonable accommodation. The essential functions are listed in order of importance.) Marketing & Communications Develop and implement marketing and communications activities that support the offices, programs, services, and strategic priorities of Partnerships & Workforce. Coordinate marketing campaigns across print, web, email, social media, presentations, and other communication channels while ensuring consistent branding and messaging. Digital Communications Maintain and update websites, digital content, social media platforms, email marketing, and other communication channels. Monitor communication metrics and recommend improvements to increase audience engagement and effectiveness. Project Coordination & Collaboration Coordinate marketing and communications projects across multiple offices within Partnerships & Workforce. Collaborate with University Marketing & Communications and campus partners to support recruitment, outreach, events, programs, and strategic initiatives. Build productive relationships with faculty, staff, students, employers, alumni, community organizations, and external stakeholders. Content Development & Storytelling Research, write, edit, and produce news stories, feature articles, web content, newsletters, reports, presentations, speeches, talking points, email communications, and social media content. Coordinate photography, video, and other multimedia content that highlights the impact of Partnerships & Workforce programs, services, partnerships, and university Administrative Support & Special Projects Track communication activities, maintain marketing calendars and content libraries, assist with vendor coordination, and support special projects as assigned. Perform other related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Education Bachelor's degree ­ Bachelor's degree from an accredited college or university in Marketing, Communications, Journalism, Public Relations, Digital Media, or a related field. Experience Intermediate (3 to 4 years job-related experience) Requires three to four years of progressively responsible experience in marketing, communications, public relations, digital communications, or a related field. Experience in higher education, nonprofit, workforce development, or public sector communications is preferred KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES Excellent written, editing, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. ­ Demonstrated ability to manage multiple projects, prioritize competing deadlines, and work effectively in a fast-paced environment. ­ Strong writing and storytelling skills with the ability to develop content for print, web, presentations, newsletters, email campaigns, and social media. ­ Ability to build collaborative relationships with faculty, staff, students, university leadership, and external partners. ­ Experience managing websites, content management systems, social media platforms, email marketing, and digital communications. ­ Strong organizational, analytical, problem-solving, and project coordination skills with exceptional attention to detail. ­ Proficiency with Microsoft Office and marketing and communications technologies. Experience with graphic design, photography, videography, analytics tools, and emerging communications technologies preferred. School/College/Division: H92 - VP Student Affairs Primary department: H9209 - International Students & Scholars (H9209) Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Staff/Administrative Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Hourly rate: Salary minimum: TBD Salary hire maximum: TBD Working conditions: ­- Normal office environment.\n­- May require occasional evening and weekend work, as well as limited travel on and off campus to support events, programs, and community engagement activities. Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3607?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "University Counselor I/II",
      "description": "University Counselor I/II | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) University Counselor I/II Detroit, MI, United States | req3607 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/10/2026 Back to Search University Counselor I/II Wayne State University is searching for an experienced University Counselor I/II at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): Serve as a Designated School Official (DSO) for F-1 students and an Alternative Responsible Officer (ARO) for J-1 exchange visitors, ensuring compliance with federal immigration regulations and SEVIS requirements. Issue and maintain Form I-20s and DS-2019s and ensure accurate and timely maintenance of student and scholar immigration records. Provide comprehensive immigration advising to F-1 and J-1 students and scholars on maintaining status, travel, employment authorization, program changes, dependents, and other immigration matters. Advise students and scholars on complex immigration matters involving federal regulations, University policies, and individual circumstances. Counsel F-1 students regarding CPT, OPT, STEM OPT, on-campus employment, and other employment-related requirements. Counsel J-1 scholars regarding employment authorization, program requirements, status maintenance, and applicable Department of State regulations. Prepare and file H-1B petitions on behalf of eligible University employees and scholars, ensuring compliance with applicable USCIS requirements and deadlines. Coordinate with the University Immigration Attorney on permanent residence matters, including preparation and coordination of University-sponsored employment-based permanent residence petitions. Review immigration applications, petitions, supporting documentation, and requests for accuracy, completeness, and regulatory compliance. Interpret and apply complex and frequently changing regulations and guidance issued by USCIS, DHS, SEVP, and the Department of State. Monitor federal regulatory and policy changes and assess their impact on University students, scholars, employees, and OISS operations. Identify potential immigration compliance issues and provide appropriate guidance, escalation, and resolution in consultation with OISS leadership and legal counsel when necessary. Maintain accurate records and ensure timely reporting and updates in SEVIS and other University systems. Collaborate with academic departments, Human Resources, faculty, administrators, and other University offices to resolve immigration, employment, and compliance matters. Provide individual advising through appointments, email, telephone, and other communication channels while managing a high volume of inquiries and cases. Conduct orientations, workshops, presentations, and training programs for international students, scholars, faculty, staff, and University departments. Develop and maintain immigration advising materials, communications, webpages, presentations, and other resources to ensure accurate dissemination of regulatory information. Support the recruitment, enrollment, retention, and academic success of international students and scholars through timely and effective advising and support services. Participate in the development and implementation of OISS policies, procedures, and processes in response to changes in federal regulations and University priorities. Maintain confidentiality and exercise professional judgment when handling sensitive immigration, employment, academic, and personal information. Participate in professional development and maintain current knowledge of international education practices and federal immigration regulations. Perform other related duties and special projects assigned by OISS leadership. Qualifications: Education: Bachelor’s (Level 1) or Master’s (Level II) degree in international studies,\ninternational education, social sciences, counseling, or a related field from\nan accredited college or university. Candidates with an equivalent combination\nof education and relevant professional experience may be considered. Experience: Requires a minimum of one year of professional experience in international\nstudent and/or scholar advising, immigration advising, or a closely related\nfield. Demonstrated knowledge of current U.S. immigration laws, regulations,\nand procedures governing F-1, J-1, H-1B, and employment-based permanent\nresidence is required. Experience with SEVIS, immigration petition preparation,\nand advising within a higher education environment is strongly preferred. School/College/Division: H92 - VP Student Affairs Primary department: H9209 - International Students & Scholars (H9209) Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Academic/Faculty Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Hourly rate: Salary minimum: TBD Salary hire maximum: TBD Working conditions: Office environment Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3610?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Research Assistant, Population Sciences - Department of Oncology",
      "description": "Research Assistant, Population Sciences - Department of Oncology | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Research Assistant, Population Sciences - Department of Oncology Detroit, MI, United States | req3610 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/10/2026 Back to Search Research Assistant, Population Sciences - Department of Oncology Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Research Assistant, Population Sciences - Department of Oncology at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): Support\n  the design and implementation of clinical research protocols involving human\n  subjects. Recruit and screen study participants through approved methods,\n  including outreach, surveys, and interviews. Conduct study visits and collect\n  clinical and research data in compliance with Institutional Review Board\n  (IRB), regulatory, and protocol requirements. Communicate regularly with the\n  Principal Investigator and research team regarding study progress and\n  participant status. Collect, enter, and maintain\n  accurate clinical and research data, including outcomes, surveys, and case\n  report forms. Monitor data quality and completeness. Perform preliminary data\n  analysis and generate graphs, charts, and tables to summarize results in\n  accordance with study protocols. Proofread and verify research data and\n  documentation for accuracy. Prepare\n  and assist with presentations of research findings for professional,\n  scientific, and academic conferences. Organize and summarize study results\n  for submission to peer-reviewed journals. Assist in drafting, factchecking,\n  editing, and proofreading scientific manuscripts, study reports, and\n  regulatory documents. Maintain organized research records and study files\n  in accordance with regulatory and institutional requirements. Coordinate\n  study materials and supplies, and support documentation for audits,\n  monitoring visits, and regulatory submissions. Ensure workspaces remain\n  organized and compliant with confidentiality and data security standards. Perform\n  other related duties as assigned. Unique duties: Qualifications: Education :  Bachelor's degree Bachelor’s degree in a related field of study, preferred.    Requires advanced knowledge predominantly intellectual in character in a field of science or learning acquired by a prolonged course of specialized intellectual instruction. Experience : Entry level (less that a year of job-related experience) ­Experience conducting clinical or human subject research is preferred. ­Experience with participant recruitment, informed consent, data collection, and maintenance of regulatory documentation is preferred Knowledge, Skills and Abilities : ­Ability to work independently and manage multiple clinical research activities while adhering to study protocols, regulatory requirements, and timelines. ­Ability to analyze, summarize, and interpret clinical research data and communicate findings effectively in reports and presentations. ­Requires knowledge predominantly intellectual in character in a field of science or learning acquired through specialized instruction. ­Knowledge of scientific integrity principles, responsible conduct of research, and human subject protections (e.g., IRB requirements, confidentiality standards). ­Excellent time management, organizational, and documentation skills. ­Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with demonstrated ability to interact professionally with research participants, clinical staff, and multidisciplinary research teams. ­Proficiency in Microsoft Office software (Word, Excel, PowerPoint) and familiarity with clinical research databases or electronic data capture systems is preferred. ­Ability to recruit and communicate effectively with diverse populations, including medically underserved communities. Preferred qualifications: ­ School/College/Division: H06 - School of Medicine Primary department: H0632 - Oncology Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Research Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Working conditions: Typical work environment may include a laboratory and/or office environment. May require working non-standard business hours\nincluding evenings and weekends. May be required to travel to scientific conferences. Job openings: Number of openings: 2 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3604?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor Tenured Faculty Appointment",
      "description": "Associate/Full Professor Tenured Faculty Appointment | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Associate/Full Professor Tenured Faculty Appointment Detroit, MI, United States | req3604 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/9/2026 Back to Search Associate/Full Professor Tenured Faculty Appointment Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Associate/Full Professor Tenured Faculty Appointment at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Position Purpose: The College of Education (COE) at Wayne State University (WSU) is pleased to invite applications for a tenured faculty position at the rank of Associate Professor or Full Professor .  Salary and rank for position are commensurate with qualifications and experience. Application Deadline: October 16, 2026. Start Date: Fall 2027 The Wayne State University College of Education invites applications for a full-time, 9-month, tenured faculty position in the History of Education and/or Cultural Studies in Education, with a research agenda centered on the study of Black education broadly defined. This innovative role will include graduate teaching and advising responsibilities in the COE Educational Leadership and Policy Studies (ELPS) program, inform and assist in the development and oversight of curriculum, youth and professional development, and school-community engagement programs in coordination with the newly established WSU Detroit Center for Black Studies. The College of Education: Since beginning as the Detroit Normal Training School in 1881, the College of Education at Wayne State University has grown to offer award-winning, nationally recognized bachelor’s, master’s, education specialist, and doctoral degree programs in 29 programs with 39 degree and certificate options, which are accredited through their national professional organizations and by the Michigan Department of Education. The College of Education educates approximately 2,000 students annually. The College’s diverse academic programs benefit greatly from its history of engagement with urban communities and the comprehensive range of programs spanning teaching and research opportunities afforded by its location in metropolitan Detroit. The College of Education’s faculty and staff are dedicated to preparing professionals who can contribute in meaningful ways to a global, technology-oriented society by helping them acquire the knowledge, skills and attitudes necessary to succeed in their chosen careers in education, health, wellness, sports, counseling, government, and more. The Wayne State University Community: Wayne State University is classified as a “doctoral university: very high research activity” by the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education. We are located in Midtown Detroit, the city center and one of the country’s fastest growing neighborhoods, which welcomes more than 2.5 million visitors annually. Here, you’ll find 200 acres of green space, walking paths, and state-of-the-art facilities surrounded by renowned cultural institutions and industry-leading employers. Candidates should possess scholarship that demonstrates a sustained commitment to advancing the study of Black education while contributing to broader conversations in the history of education, cultural studies, educational foundations, curriculum studies, and related interdisciplinary fields. Essential Functions : Exceptional candidates are humanities-oriented scholars whose research examines the historical, cultural, political, intellectual, and institutional dimensions of Black educational experiences in the United States and/or across the African Diaspora. Areas of specialization may include, but are not limited to: Histories of Black education and educational thought. Curriculum, pedagogy, and the politics of knowledge production. Black cultural studies and education. Black educational politics, policy, and social movements. Arts, humanities, and cultural production as sites of Black teaching and learning. Black independent schools and institution-building traditions. Informal, community-based, and out-of-school educational practices. Homeschooling, home education, and alternative educational formations within Black communities. Black abolitionist, and liberatory educational traditions. Afro-Pessimist, Black feminist, Africana, and other critical theoretical approaches to education. Rehumanizing, culturally sustaining, and community-centered methodologies in Black educational research. Interdisciplinary approaches that expand conventional understandings of schooling, education, knowledge, and Black intellectual life. Minimum Qualifications: A Record of Community-Engaged Research and Curriculum : We seek a scholar who has established a community-engaged and Black Studies-focused research agenda, preferably through works supporting the work of urban schools and cultural institutions, including museums and youth & community organizations. Scholars who have engaged in humanities-focused educational and curriculum initiatives and with a track record of ongoing engagement with educators, youth, community leaders and organizers, and/or cultural workers will be prioritized. Scholarly Productivity: Compelling candidates will have publications in reputable academic journals in education, humanities, and/or cultural studies, monographs, edited volumes, and and/or journal special issues who are engaged in multi- and trans-disciplinary research and collaborations both within and beyond education. They will also be engaged in dissemination practices that raise the visibility of Black Studies in Education (e.g., publications in influential journals and/or media outlets, state- or national-level speaking engagements, community-based programming with notable impact, etc.). Doctoral Advising: As the College of Education moves to establish a new Ph.D. program, applicants must have experience and an interest in and capacity to advise doctoral students early in their appointment, especially to guide and support Ph.D. and Ed.D. students already enrolled in the ELPS program. Embody College Values: Important review criteria include evidence of a commitment to working with communities and organizations that reflect Detroit’s demographics and address their socio-cultural, economic, and political realities; commitment to excellence in research, teaching, and graduate student advising; and eagerness to embody the College’s vivid vision of radical neighborliness (i.e., supporting all members of the College and local communities by taking bold and provocative actions to foster compassion, inclusivity, social connection, and a sense of the common good). Applicants must have an earned doctorate in Education, Black or African/African American Studies, a disciplinary or interdisciplinary field in the humanities, or a related field. Application Process : All applicants must use the WSU Online Hiring System at www. https://hr.wayne.edu/jobs , referring to position req # 3604 . Electronic applications must include a cover letter; curriculum vita (CV); names and contact information for at least 3 references, and 2 to 3 representative publications. The search committee will begin reviewing applications on October 16, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries about the position may be directed to the chair of the search committee: Dr. Rema Vassar Professor, Educational Leadership and Policy Studies College of Education DrRema@wayne.edu Preferred Qualifications: Educational Experience : We are especially interested in applicants who have served as educators in K-20 schools, community-based organizations, or cultural institutions, particularly in urban and diverse settings. Externally Funded Research : We are excited about applicants who have demonstrated the ability to raise external funds to support their research and other collaborative projects. School/College/Division: H02 - College of Education Primary Department: H0252 - Administrative & Organizational Stu Employment Type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Academic/Faculty Funding/Salary Information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Annual Salary:  To be determined Working Conditions: Normal faculty environment. Job Openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3606?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Autopsy Attendant - Medical Examiner's Office",
      "description": "Autopsy Attendant - Medical Examiner's Office | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Autopsy Attendant - Medical Examiner's Office Detroit, MI, United States | req3606 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/9/2026 Back to Search Autopsy Attendant - Medical Examiner's Office Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Autopsy Attendant - Medical Examiner's Office at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): Job Purpose: Performs technical work in support of the Pathologist during autopsies and other postmortem examinations. Assistance to the pathologist involves opening the body, evisceration, reconstruction of the body after the autopsy, collecting toxicological and other body fluid samples during autopsies; arranging x-rays and photographs of human remains; transporting bodies from cooler to table; storage of samples; clean up and maintenance of the morgue, supplies and evidence and maintaining a catalogue of the morgue inventory.  Reports to Lab Manager. Essential Functions: Provides assistance to Pathologist by opening the body, eviscerating, and reconstructing the body after the autopsy, collecting toxicological and other body fluid samples during autopsies; arranging x-rays and photographs of human remains; transporting bodies from cooler to table; storage of samples; clean up and maintenance of the morgue, supplies and evidence and maintaining a catalogue of the morgue inventory. Releases bodies to funeral homes and maintains the proper chain of custody with regard to the human remains and any clothing, personal effects, jewelry, etc. that may accompany it. Ensures proper handling of any paperwork associated with receiving or releasing bodies. Prepares morgue for autopsies by laying out supplies and instruments, transferring body to autopsy table, undressing and assisting the residents with recording description of the body. Prepares body for autopsy; weighs and measures human remains. Assist with training new hires. May remove organs or tissue; sutures and cleans body upon completion of autopsy. Ensures proper handling, transfers, labeling and storage of specimens. Cleans and maintains work area and equipment using sanitation/decontamination, safety and biohazard control measures. Cleans and disinfects the cooler, carts, floor space, surface areas, etc., as needed. Ensures the environs of the space are maintained, including slings, linens, cadaver covers, and frames. Ensures the temperature of the cooler is maintained and within normal limits. Addresses repair issues with engineering or vendors as needed. Monitors level of supplies, conducting inventories and ordering supplies and materials as needed. Prepares human remains for identification photographs by shrouding body with a white cloth, exposing just the face and placing decedent case number under the chin. Utilizes camera to take and upload overhead photo into computer. Coordinates additional autopsy photographs as well as storage of photos with Biomedical Photographer. Performs other related duties as assigned. Unique duties: Qualifications: Education: High school diploma or equivalent or an equivalent combination of education and experience demonstrating the ability to perform the work. Some college coursework in anatomy & physiology is preferred. Experience: A minimum of 6 months of experience handling bodies in a morgue, mortuary or similar setting. Some knowledge of human anatomy preferred. Some knowledge of and/or experience working in a morgue, hospital or laboratory setting. Ability and willingness to work with human cadavers and anatomical specimens. Ability to use electronic power saw and various other medical equipment. Ability to transport and place human cadavers in appropriate locations. Typically, incumbents possess knowledge and experience related to morgue operations and laboratory procedures. Experience in medical-legal death investigation or forensic science is preferred. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Requires the ability regularly to lift 50 lbs. or more. knowledge of physiology/anatomy; or any combination of education or experience which would demonstrate the ability to perform the work. Knowledge of safety and biohazard control measures; use and operation of photographic equipment; cleaning and sanitizing practices. The ability to follow oral and written directions and protocols; deal with death and human remains in various stages of decomposition, disease and trauma; handle the psychological effects of death; maintain the confidentiality and integrity of information; adhere to stringent sanitation and biohazard control measures and communicate effectively both orally and in writing. Preferred qualifications: School/College/Division: H06 - School of Medicine Primary department: H0689 - Medical Examiner's Office Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Staff/Administrative Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Hourly rate: Salary minimum: $47,109.00 Salary hire maximum:  $56,527.00 Working conditions: The Medical Examiner’s Office is a 24 hour per day, 7 day per week operation.\nWorking weekends and holidays is required.\nOvertime may also be required. Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Equal employment opportunity statement: Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans. Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3605?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Economics - Macroeconomics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Economics - Macroeconomics | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor of Economics - Macroeconomics Detroit, MI, United States | req3605 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/9/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor of Economics - Macroeconomics Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Assistant Professor of Economics - Macroeconomics at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): The Department of Economics at Wayne State University invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor, beginning on or about August 18, 2027. This is a full-time (9-month), benefits-eligible faculty position. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to teaching in macroeconomics and econometrics at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. The Department offers B.A., M.A., and Ph.D. programs. Candidates should demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in both research and teaching and should contribute to the academic and professional activities of the department. The teaching load is 2:2 (fall: winter semesters). WSU is classified by the Carnegie Foundation as a RU/VH (Research University, Very High research activity) institution and serves more than 24,000 students across 370 academic programs, making it a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit. Qualifications: We seek candidates specializing in macroeconomics with a strong background in applied macroeconometrics. Candidates should have an active research agenda in macroeconomics and demonstrate strong potential for high-quality research and publication. Candidates are expected to hold a Ph.D. in Economics no later than August 17, 2027. To apply, go to https://jobs.wayne.edu. An on-line application is required. Candidates must upload a cover letter, curriculum vitae, job market paper, research statement, teaching statement, and evidence of teaching effectiveness (teaching evaluations, if available). Three letters of recommendation should be sent to Liang Hu, Department Chair, lianghu@wayne.edu, and Young-Ro Yoon, Chair of the Search Committee, Department of Economics, ed7847@wayne.edu To receive full consideration, applications must be received by November 16, 2026. School/College/Division: H12 - College of Liberal Arts & Science Primary department: H0306 - Economics Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Academic/Faculty Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Working conditions: This position must be filled pursuant to the provisions of a collective bargaining agreement, and as such may be filled by a qualified bargaining unit member, should one apply. Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3602?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Director, Career Services",
      "description": "Director, Career Services | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Director, Career Services Detroit, MI, United States | req3602 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/9/2026 Back to Search Director, Career Services Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Director, Career Services at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): Job Purpose Provide strategic leadership and direction for University Career Services at Wayne State University. Lead a comprehensive, student-centered approach to career development that helps students explore career pathways, develop career-ready skills, gain meaningful career-engaged experiences, and successfully transition from college to career. Collaborate across the university and with external partners to strengthen career development, employer engagement, and connections between education and career pathways. Build partnerships with schools and colleges, student success and engagement units, enrollment and outreach programs, employers, alumni, and community partners to extend the reach and impact of career development. Serve as a key partner in advancing Wayne State's College to Career (C2C) initiative and other institutional priorities related to student success, career readiness, and workforce development Essential Functions (Essential functions are the primary duties/major job responsibilities that an employee must be able to perform, with or without reasonable accommodation. The essential functions are listed in order of importance.) Strategic Leadership and Administration - 25% Provide strategic leadership for University Career Services. Establish priorities, goals, programs, and practices aligned with university and student success priorities. Lead, supervise, develop, and evaluate staff and foster a collaborative, student-centered, high-performing team. Oversee departmental operations, budgets, resources, policies, assessment, and continuous improvement. Identify and incorporate emerging trends and best practices in career development, employer engagement, higher education, and workforce development to strengthen Career Services strategy and operations. Career Development and Student Engagement - 20% Lead the delivery of comprehensive career development services, including career advising and coaching, career exploration, career preparation, workshops, programs, and resources that support students throughout their academic journey and transition to employment or continued education. Develop strategies to increase student awareness, engagement, and utilization of career services and career-engaged learning opportunities. Advance scalable approaches to career development through technology, digital resources, peer-to-peer models, programming, and embedded partnerships. Promote equitable access to career development resources and opportunities for students across disciplines, backgrounds, and stages of their academic experience. University Partnerships and Career Integration - 15% Build collaborative partnerships with schools and colleges, faculty, academic advisors, student success and engagement units, enrollment and outreach programs, and other university partners to integrate career development and career awareness throughout the student experience. Provide consultation, resources, tools, and scalable infrastructure that support schools and colleges and other campus partners in strengthening career development and connecting students with career opportunities. Collaborate with university partners on K–12 outreach, recruitment, career exploration, and pathway initiatives that help prospective and current students understand connections among academic programs, career interests, and future opportunities. Partner across the university to advance institutional priorities related to career readiness and career-engaged learning, including internships and other experiences that connect academic learning with career goals. Employer, Alumni, and External Engagement - 15% Provide strategic leadership and oversight for employer engagement, recruiting activities, career fairs, student employment initiatives, and other programs that connect students with employment and career opportunities. Develop and strengthen relationships with employers and other external partners to expand internships, employment opportunities, career exploration, mentoring, networking, and other career-engaged experiences. Oversee Career Services' student employment programs and initiatives and collaborate with campus employers to connect students with meaningful on-campus employment opportunities. Collaborate with schools and colleges, advancement, alumni relations, and other university partners to leverage alumni and professional networks in support of student career development. Represent Wayne State University with employers, professional organizations, community partners, and other external stakeholders. Technology, Data, and Assessment - 10% Provide strategic leadership and oversight for the university's career management platform and other career technologies and digital resources to support student engagement, employer recruiting, career services delivery, data collection, and operational efficiency. Establish goals and metrics to assess student engagement, employer engagement, service utilization, post-graduation career outcomes, and program effectiveness. Use data, labor-market information, and stakeholder feedback to inform priorities and continuous improvement. Collaborate with university partners to strengthen career-related data collection, analysis, and reporting. Communication and Outreach  - 10% Develop and oversee communication and outreach strategies that increase awareness and utilization of Career Services, career resources, programs, and opportunities among students and university stakeholders. Collaborate with university partners to communicate career opportunities, student experiences, employer partnerships, and career outcomes and to extend the reach of career development resources across the university. Serve as a visible campus advocate for career development and career readiness. Perform other related duties as assigned  - 5% WORK CONTEXT (Position Scope) Job Reports to: Vice President/AVP/Dean Leadership Accountability: Develops strategic plans and interprets policy Supervisory Accountability: Supervises professionals and non-managers Organizational Accountability:  Manages department Financial Accountability:  Manages operating budget Customer Accountability:  Interfaces with officials and executives Freedom to Act: Sets broad policies and objectives Unique duties: Qualifications: MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Education: Bachelor's degree ­Preferably a Master's degree from an accredited college or university in higher education, counseling, student affairs, business, organizational leadership, human resources, education, or a related field. Experience: Intermediate (3 to 4 years job-related experience) ­Minimum of seven years of progressively responsible professional experience in career services, higher education, student success, employer relations, workforce development, talent development, or a related area. ­Demonstrated leadership and supervisory experience, including responsibility for staff, programs, operations, and organizational priorities. ­Experience developing collaborative relationships with academic units, employers, and other internal and external partners. ­Experience leading a university or college career services operation or comparable student success, talent development, or workforce-focused unit. ­Experience developing partnerships with academic units, employers, alumni, and external organizations. ­Experience with career management platforms, career technologies, labor-market tools, student employment, and data-informed program assessment. ­Experience developing scalable approaches to career development and integrating career readiness into the broader student experience. ­An equivalent combination of education and experience may be accepted. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities ­Leadership: Demonstrated ability to provide strategic and operational leadership, establish priorities, lead and develop teams, manage change, and foster a collaborative and high-performing work environment. ­Career Development: Comprehensive knowledge of contemporary career development practices, career readiness, experiential and career-engaged learning, employer recruiting practices, and workforce trends. ­Student Success: Knowledge of strategies for engaging and supporting a diverse student population and increasing access to career development resources and opportunities. ­Employer and Partnership Development: Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain productive relationships across academic and administrative units and with employers, alumni, community organizations, and other external partners. ­Technology and Data: Ability to leverage career technology, data, assessment, and labor-market information to improve programs, increase efficiency, measure outcomes, and inform decision-making. ­Communication: Excellent written, verbal, presentation, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to communicate effectively with students, faculty, administrators, employers, alumni, and community partners. ­Collaboration: Demonstrated ability to work effectively within a complex, decentralized university environment and develop collaborative approaches across multiple units and stakeholders. ­Planning and Organization: Strong organizational, project management, analytical, and problem-solving skills. Ability to manage multiple priorities and initiatives effectively. Preferred qualifications: School/College/Division: Generic Division Primary department: H9203 - Placement Services (H9203) Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Executive Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Hourly rate: Salary minimum: Salary hire maximum: 107,592 Working conditions: Working Conditions\n­Normal office environment with regular interaction with students, faculty, staff, employers, alumni, and external partners.\n­Some evening and weekend work is required to support career fairs, employer events, student programs, and university activities.\n­Occasional local, regional, and national travel may be required. Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3603?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Marketing & Communications, Graduate School",
      "description": "Associate Director, Marketing & Communications, Graduate School | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Associate Director, Marketing & Communications, Graduate School Detroit, MI, United States | req3603 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/8/2026 Back to Search Associate Director, Marketing & Communications, Graduate School Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Associate Director, Marketing & Communications, Graduate School at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Position Purpose: Elevate the Wayne State University Graduate School brand to prospective graduate students and the broader Wayne State community. Plans include marketing and communications, and graduate outreach goals and objectives outlined in the strategic plan. Supervise web/online communication and web writer and collaborate with other University staff and units within the University, as well as the community. Reports to Graduate School Dean. Essential Functions : Serves as the Graduate School’s lead communications and public relations expert, developing official communications on behalf of the college and Dean, and leading strategy for email marketing (prospective and current students), social media, advertising, and the Graduate School website. Advises the Dean and senior leadership on marketing and communications strategies that support graduate recruitment initiatives and promote signature events. Collaborate with the Central Office of Marketing and Communications to advance University goals. Supervises website and online communications staff, providing oversight of marketing and recruitment strategy across multiple media while ensuring adherence to brand standards and consistent messaging for all graduate education initiatives. Establishes expectations, assigns and delegates work, evaluates performance, prepares appraisals and professional development plans, and provides ongoing coaching and mentoring. Manages administrative personnel processes including hiring, promotions, disciplinary actions, salary adjustments, and workforce planning, and ensures timely and effective communication with staff. Develops marketing and recruitment recommendations for Wayne State University graduate programs in collaboration with Schools and Colleges. Provides oversight of new and existing outreach and graduate recruitment efforts in consultation with the Dean, senior leadership, and the Director of Admissions. Oversee the Graduate School’s Professional Development Series, Graduate Ambassadors Program, and related initiatives. Build relationships with graduate students, the Graduate Student Association, and postdoctoral scholars to foster a strong sense of community. Administers the Graduate School marketing budget, including procurement of branded apparel and promotional materials for major on campus events such as FestiFall, recruitment activities, and campus visits. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Education : Master’s degree Bachelor’s degree from an accredited college or university in Marketing, Communications or related field. Master’s degree is strongly preferred. Experience : Expert (established subject matter expert, 7+ years’ experience). 5-8 demonstrated experience in a marketing and communications role, preferably in higher education. Proven track record of marketing management and strategic planning experience, and in developing and administering a marketing/communications program. At least 2 years of supervisory experience is required. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities : Ability to set and implement a self- directed agenda. Consensus building skills. Excellent ability to prioritize work assignments to meet deadlines. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with all levels of management in client organizations throughout the University. Ability to plan and set strategic direction for marketing and communication efforts and the ability to work and lead a variety of people and situations. Ability to articulate the mission of Wayne State University and the Graduate School to many constituencies. Establish and maintain positive working relationships with others, both internally and externally, to achieve the goals of the organization. Demonstrated ability to work with all levels of management and educational administrators within and outside the University. Ability to prioritize and plan work activities and develop strategies to achieve organizational goals, develop realistic action plans. Ability to manage multiple projects and priorities simultaneously, often under tight deadlines. Must be a self-starter and highly organized. Ability to identify and resolve problems, develop alternative approaches and meet challenges with resourcefulness. Ability to evaluate, interpret, and present complex information effectively in professionally prepared documents or presentations. Must possess a demonstrated attention to detail in order to achieve a high degree of accuracy. Ability to develop new and unique ways to improve operations, achieve established goals and objectives, and create new opportunities. Ability to anticipate, understand, and respond to the needs of internal, external and prospective stakeholders to meet or exceed their expectations within the University parameters. Ability to speak, listen and write in a clear, thorough and timely manner using appropriate and effective communication tools and techniques. Able to effectively present information to individuals and groups, communicating effectively orally and in writing, as appropriate for the needs of the audience. Able to work with teams collaboratively and manage change in a large organization. Demonstrated ability to lead and foster a cooperative team-focused work environment. Awareness of multicultural issues and a broad knowledge of diversity dimensions. Able and willing to serve and engage others in a multicultural environment. Strong copy writing, editing and proofreading skills. High level of proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite, Adobe, data management systems, Marketing and Publications software and Windows based computer applications. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree is strongly preferred. School/College/Division: H11 - Graduate School Primary Department: H1102 - Graduate School Employment Type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Staff/Administrative Funding/Salary Information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Annual Salary:  To be determined Working Conditions: Normal office environment. Occasional travel on and off campus. Occasional weekends/evenings. Job Openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Background Check Requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
