    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3595?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Senior Major Gift Officer – Mike Ilitch School of Business",
      "description": "Senior Major Gift Officer – Mike Ilitch School of Business | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Senior Major Gift Officer – Mike Ilitch School of Business Detroit, MI, United States | req3595 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/3/2026 Back to Search Senior Major Gift Officer – Mike Ilitch School of Business Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Senior Major Gift Officer – Mike Ilitch School of Business at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): JOB PURPOSE Provide expertise and experience while participating in fundraising efforts, strategies, and initiatives for the Mike Ilitch School of Business (MISB). Manage a prospect portfolio to achieve ambitious fundraising and contact goals annually. Cultivate, solicit, and steward major gift donors to meet established fundraising goals within the allocated expense budget. This position reports to the Senior Director, Major Gifts – Mike Ilitch School of Business. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS (Essential functions are the primary duties/major job responsibilities that an employee must be able to perform, with or without reasonable accommodation. The essential functions are listed in order of importance.) % Time Serve in the role of seasoned professional by providing expertise and guidance in identifying fundraising needs and priorities. Collaborate with the AVP and Sr. Director of Major Gifts, Dean, Department Chairs, and faculty. Develop major gift fundraising plans and strategies. Establish realistic and achievable timeline. Remain current with fundraising trends.  25% Manage a prospect portfolio to achieve ambitious fundraising and contact goals annually. Establish and implement prospect qualification, cultivation, and solicitation strategies with a particular focus on securing commitments of $25,000+. Engage university leadership and college-based colleagues as appropriate to support prospect strategies. 25% Responsible for achieving annual fundraising goal and assisting in meeting unit and division goals; allocating and prioritizing time and resources to achieve the greatest return on investment. Performance and productivity will be measured on the following core metrics as determined annually: Dollars raised for campaign gift commitments. Number of major gift solicitations of $25,000+ Number of major gift closures at $25,000+ Number of face-to-face prospect and donor meetings (in-person and virtual visits) Number of meaningful, significant contacts each month 20% Serve as seasoned resource with Development units (e.g., Foundation Relations, Donor Experience, Planned Giving, and Prospect Development) as well as other university units to ensure operational efficiencies within the unit.15% Draft and negotiate gift and pledge documentation ranging from moderate to high complexity. Ensure timely routing of donor contributions to central development for processing. 10% Perform other related duties as assigned. 5% WORK CONTEXT This grid characterizes the position scope. Job Reports to: Senior Director/Chair Leadership Accountability: Develops strategic plans and interprets policy Supervisory Accountability: None Organizational Accountability: None Financial Accountability: Monitors expenditures Customer Accountability: Interfaces with customers outside the S/C/D Freedom to Act: Operates with significant autonomy Unique duties: Qualifications: MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Education   Bachelor's degree Graduation from an accredited college or university with a degree in marketing, public relations, business administration or related field. Experience   Specialist (minimum 5 years of job-related experience) Five (5) to Eight (8) years of experience working as a fundraising professional in direct major donor solicitations in excess of $25,000 or equivalent experience as a fundraising professional at another institution operating in a similar capacity. Direct experience preparing proposals and conducting major gift solicitations required. Higher education development and prior volunteer management experience preferred. Professional understanding of, and interest in, Development and Alumni Affairs best practices and general operations. Understanding of trends and best practices related to major gift solicitation. Positive and collaborative professional philosophy, recognizing that this position is integral to the achievement of the goals and objectives of Development and Alumni Affairs. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities ANALYTICAL AND PRESENTATION SKILLS : Ability to evaluate, interpret, and present complex information effectively  in professionally prepared documents or presentations. Must possess a demonstrated attention to detail in order to achieve a high degree of accuracy. COLLABORATION: Exhibits a willingness to partner with units across the university with integrity and high character to reach unified goals. Possesses the ability to navigate difficult situations with grace and understanding. COMMUNICATIONS SKILLS: Ability to communicate clearly, concisely and professionally both orally and in writing. Strong interpersonal skills are a must. Demonstrates the ability to plan and deliver oral and written communications that are impactful and persuasive including presentations to university management and committees. LEARNING AGILITY : Independent and innovative self-starter who displays an ability to learn quickly and easily adapts to changing situations. Experience researching topics to gain knowledge and understanding when information gaps exist. PLANNING AND ORGANIZATION: Ability to manage several projects simultaneously, prioritize and plan work activities while meeting respective deadlines. Strong organizational and time-management skills required. Maturity, sound judgment and ability to handle multiple tasks simultaneously in a deadline-oriented environment. TEAMWORK : Ability to collaborate effectively with colleagues within the division and other units to gain full understanding of the project and desired outcome; ability to produce high quality and consistent results with assistance of team members; provide support to teammates when tackling complex projects. TECHNICAL SKILLS: Proficient in the use of complex administrative systems software, preferrably in the higher education environment. Proficient with Microsoft Office suite, especially Word, Excel and Teams. Ability to understand and translate complex concepts into lay terms. Working Conditions Normal office environment. Adhere to confidentiality and data usage policy for the division. This position requires a high level of commitment and enthusiasm. Due to travel requirements, evening meetings and special events, etc., candidate must be willing to work extended hours as needed. Hybrid work schedule Preferred qualifications: School/College/Division: H86 - Development & Alumni Affairs Primary department: H8605-Development Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Staff/Administrative Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Hourly rate: Salary minimum: $82,000 Salary hire maximum: $88,000 Working conditions: Hybrid work schedule\nNormal working conditions Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3589?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "University Counselor I/II – College to Career Specialist Position Overview",
      "description": "University Counselor I/II – College to Career Specialist Position Overview | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) University Counselor I/II – College to Career Specialist Position Overview Detroit, MI, United States | req3589 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/2/2026 Back to Search University Counselor I/II – College to Career Specialist Position Overview Wayne State University is searching for an experienced University Counselor I/II – College to Career Specialist Position Overview at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): Position Overview The University Counselor I/II – College to Career Specialist plays a crucial role in guiding students in a variety of career-related and student development practices including self-discovery and reflection, understanding the world of work, experiential learning, tapping into social networks, advanced education, on-campus employment, part-time and full-time jobs. The University Counselor I/II – College to Career Specialist will utilize knowledge of career theories, current research, and best practices in the knowledge domains of student development, career development, student learning, decision making, learning styles, social identity development, leadership, identity development, counseling processes, evaluation tools, social media and AI tools, and job/internship search processes. Additionally, the University Counselor I/II – College to Career Specialist will support the goals of Wayne State University's Strategic Plan, the College to Career (C2C) Initiative, and Student Success 2.0 through the delivery of career development services that promote student engagement, career readiness, experiential learning, and post-graduation success. The University Counselor I/II – College to Career Specialist will work closely with the Manager of Student Engagement and the Manager of Employer Engagement to increase student participation through programming, career readiness initiatives, experiential learning opportunities, and employer engagement activities. Position Description Career Counseling/Coaching (50%) Serve as a Career Services liaison for WSU schools and colleges as assigned. Contribute to student learning, development, and enhanced educational experiences through working closely with students to help them better understand the process of making thoughtful career decisions. Engage in counseling, advising, and coaching with clients at all phases of their career development. Demonstrate excellence in career coaching by helping students develop career readiness competencies, connect academic experiences to career pathways, and successfully transition from college to career. Knowledge of Handshake and promotion of the platform as a recruiting and career engagement tool that connects students with employers, enables appointment scheduling with University Counselor I/II College to Career Specialists, and facilitates participation in networking and career development events. Create and implement activities and events that support the College to Career student journey model and advance the goals of the College to Career Initiative, Student Success 2.0, and Wayne State University's Strategic Plan. Use and develop resource expertise to educate students regarding access to career information to aid their career planning and job/internship search. Work with students to identify experiences that are meaningful to them and encourage participation in student organizations, community service, employment, research or creative projects, and experiential learning opportunities across multiple sectors. Develop innovative ways of bringing the world of work to students to help inform their career thinking and planning. Use current technology such as LinkedIn and Generative AI to demonstrate to students how to effectively leverage these tools. Stay informed about emerging technologies and assist Career Services in remaining at the forefront of innovation. Assist students in understanding and reflecting on the transferability of their academic coursework as they explore career and educational pathways. Support students in implementing their career and educational choices through resume development, interviewing strategies, job-search techniques, networking, employer and graduate school selection, and offer negotiation. Promote the highest standards of the profession in accordance with the principles, ethics, and standards of NACE, Midwest ACE, ACPA, NASPA, and established Student Affairs best practices. Help students understand the importance of self-awareness and reflection in translating educational experiences into meaningful career pathways and lifelong career management. Design and implement initiatives utilizing the College to Career Initiative mission and research to maximize participation among students, campus partners, employers, and community stakeholders. Workshop Facilitation and Events (15%) Create and present interactive programs and workshops that engage diverse student populations across the Schools and Colleges. Utilize technology and presentation/facilitation best practices to engage audiences and ensure workshop objectives and learning outcomes are achieved. Facilitate small-group dialogues around identified career topics, demonstrating effective group facilitation and leadership skills. Support Career Services events such as Career Fairs, Career Treks, Etiquette Dinners, and related initiatives. Career Technology and Student Engagement (20%) Career Readiness Technologies (e.g., Big Interview) Develop and implement strategies to increase student engagement with career readiness technologies and online career preparation resources, including the current platform, Big Interview. Manage account access and user support for career readiness technology platforms. Serve as the primary liaison with technology vendors and product support teams to address operational needs, platform enhancements, and service issues. Monitor platform utilization and prepare semester-based reports on engagement, usage, and outcomes. Collaborate with Career Services staff and campus partners to promote effective use of career readiness technologies that support interview preparation, job search strategies, and career development. Career & Workforce Insights (e.g., Steppingblocks) Develop and implement strategies to increase student, faculty, and staff engagement with career data, workforce intelligence, and career outcomes technology platforms, including the current platform, Steppingblocks. Promote the use of labor market information, career outcomes data, workforce trends, and analytics to support career exploration, informed decision-making, and career readiness initiatives. Support university-wide collaboration with academic colleges, departments, and campus partners to leverage career and workforce data in advancing student success, retention, career readiness, and post-graduation outcomes. Develop presentations, reports, dashboards, and educational materials utilizing career outcomes data, labor market intelligence, and workforce analytics. Monitor technology utilization and prepare semester-based reports assessing engagement, impact, and student outcomes. Participate in vendor training and remain informed regarding emerging trends, platform enhancements, and best practices in career technology and workforce analytics. Train and support Career Services staff, campus partners, faculty, and students in the effective use of career data and workforce analytics tools and resources. Support institutional initiatives, including Career-to-Career (C2C) and other strategic workforce development efforts, through the application of career outcomes data, labor market intelligence, and technology-enabled solutions. Data Management (15%) Maintain accurate and timely records and statistical information on clients using Handshake and other relevant tools. Analyze and communicate data trends that support operational effectiveness and student success outcomes. Qualifications: Master’s degree in Student Affairs, Counseling, Social Work, or a related field that includes the study of career development, student development, counseling, multicultural counseling, research, and assessment. Minimum 3-5 years of experience counseling, coaching, or advising college students in one-on-one and/or small-group settings, preferably in career development-related areas (including graduate assistantships, internships, or related experiences). Experience utilizing technology, social media platforms, and information management systems for service delivery. Experience presenting, facilitating, and teaching both small and large groups. Broad understanding of the world of work and current industry and workforce trends. Desired Qualifications Experience working in a fast-paced office environment. Experience administering and interpreting career assessment instruments (e.g., Strong Interest Inventory, MBTI, CliftonStrengths). Three or more years of professional experience in career services, academic advising, counseling, student affairs, workforce development, or a related field preferred. School/College/Division: H92 - VP Student Affairs Primary department: H9203 - Placement Services Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Academic/Faculty Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Hourly rate: Salary minimum: TBD Salary hire maximum: TBD Working conditions: Office environment Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3591?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Nurse Practitioner, Clinical Ryan White Program - Dept. Internal Medicine",
      "description": "Nurse Practitioner, Clinical Ryan White Program - Dept. Internal Medicine | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Nurse Practitioner, Clinical Ryan White Program - Dept. Internal Medicine Detroit, MI, United States | req3591 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/2/2026 Back to Search Nurse Practitioner, Clinical Ryan White Program - Dept. Internal Medicine Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Nurse Practitioner, Clinical Ryan White Program - Dept. Internal Medicine at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): Job Purpose: The Nurse Practitioner, Clinical position is primarily responsible for providing outpatient clinical care for patients as well as coordinating services and activities within the Wayne State University Adult HIV program.  As a member of the interdisciplinary care team the nurse practitioner collaborates with physicians, nurses, medical assistants, social workers, medical case managers, patient advocates and peer navigators.  Develops and ensures compliance with program policies and data collection.  Leads quality improvement projects within the program and conducts ongoing program evaluation. Supports re-linkage to care initiatives for persons out of care or lost to care. Reports to the Nursing Team Supervisor. Essential Functions: Clinical Care. Performs appropriate clinical care of patients in the program in the clinic.  Performs care in accordance with the standards of the clinic and federal guidelines.  Documents care appropriately in the medical record. Engages in ongoing professional development Program development.  Working with the Principal Investigator (PI) and clinical leads, develops innovative care models to increase viral suppression among patients. Emphasis on collaborative care model and team building and maintenance as well as strong networking skills. Program evaluation. Develops and implement program evaluation systems and quality improvement projects in accordance with funder and program needs. May participate in clinical research. Perform other duties as required. Unique duties: Qualifications: Education :   Master's degree Graduation from an accredited school of nursing, with Michigan licensure to practice as a nurse practitioner or physician assistant. BSN preferred. Experience: Experienced (minimum 2 years of job-related experience) Staff supervision experience. One to two years of nursing experience required with related research experience in designated area of work preferred. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Effective verbal and written communication skills. Ability to build and maintain working relationships with internal and external partners. Demonstrated problem solving and critical thinking skills. Strong organizational, time management and planning skills. Ability to be flexible and adaptable change. Preferred qualifications: School/College/Division: H06 - School of Medicine Primary department: H0609 - Internal Medicine Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Fractional Time Job category: Staff/Administrative Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Hourly rate: Salary minimum: TBD Salary hire maximum: TBD Working conditions: Office setting with regular visits to external sites and fieldwork.   Must be flexible with time availability. Job openings: Number of openings: 2 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3587?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor & Open Rank Position (Tenure-track) - School of Social Work",
      "description": "Assistant Professor & Open Rank Position (Tenure-track) - School of Social Work | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor & Open Rank Position (Tenure-track) - School of Social Work Detroit, MI, United States | req3587 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/1/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor & Open Rank Position (Tenure-track) - School of Social Work Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Assistant Professor & Open Rank Position (Tenure-track) - School of Social Work at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): Wayne State University is searching for up to two (2) tenure-track or tenured faculty members, one at open rank and one at the rank of Assistant Professor in the School of Social Work. The School of Social Work at Wayne State University invites applications for up to two full-time, tenure-track or tenured faculty positions to begin August 2027: one open rank (Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor), and one at the rank of Assistant Professor. We welcome applications from all qualified candidates whose research and practice advance the well-being of urban communities like Detroit. We are particularly interested in scholars whose work emphasizes one or more of the following areas: behavioral health and the criminal legal system (e.g., crisis response, diversion, juvenile justice, and reentry); eco-social work (e.g., trauma-informed disaster response, environmental organizing, & nature-based interventions); global social work (e.g., immigrant and refugee communities, human rights, sustainable development goals); and health (e.g., gerontology, substance use, disability, policy). Applicants need not fit within a single area and may work across them. The Wayne State University School of Social Work, accredited since 1945, is situated in a dynamic urban setting in Midtown, the heart of Detroit's Cultural and Historic District, and is committed to the urban mission of social work practice. The School offers BSW, MSW, and PhD programs and is home to two centers: the Center for Behavioral Health and Justice, which supports communities, organizations, and behavioral health and criminal-legal agencies through the implementation of evidence-based practices, and the Center for Social Work Research, which supports faculty research, scholarship, community engagement, and interdisciplinary collaboration. More information can be found at: socialwork.wayne.edu. Faculty members in the School of Social Work contribute to the School's research, teaching, and service missions. The successful candidates will be expected to conduct independent research, including maintaining an active record of peer-reviewed publication and pursuing external funding; teach across the School's BSW, MSW, and/or PhD curricula; advise and mentor students; and participate in service assignments of importance to the School, the University, and the profession. Qualifications: An earned doctorate in social work or a related field (e.g., anthropology, psychology, political science, criminal justice, geography, public health, or sociology). Applicants who have entered doctoral candidacy (ABD) will be considered; the dissertation must be filed by the appointment start date for appointment to the Assistant Professor rank. Record of scholarly publications (published, in press, or under review). Academic accomplishments commensurate with appointment at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor rank. Applications must be made online at jobs.wayne.edu. Applicants should upload: (a) a curriculum vitae; (b) a two-page cover letter describing your research interests and trajectory, your teaching philosophy, and the areas of the School's BSW, MSW, or PhD curricula you are prepared to teach; and (c) the names and full contact information of three references. For full consideration, applications should be received by September 21, 2026; review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the positions are filled. Questions may be directed to Dr. Bryan Victor, Search Committee Chair (bvictor@wayne.edu). Preferred qualifications: An MSW from a CSWE-accredited institution with 2 years post-MSW practice experience. An independent research trajectory. For candidates at the Assistant Professor rank, demonstrated potential for external funding; for candidates at the Associate or Full Professor rank, a record of secured external funding. Strong analytic ability (e.g., data science, quantitative, qualitative and/or mixed methods analysis); experience in primary data collection. Teaching experience and expertise in BSW, MSW, or PhD curricular areas. Experience with community-engaged research or community-engaged teaching. A track record of interpersonal skills that facilitate communication and connection bridging communities and multiple disciplines (e.g., social science, nursing, health sciences, engineering, humanities). School/College/Division: H13 - School of Social Work Primary department: H13 - School of Social Work Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Academic/Faculty Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Working conditions: This position must be filled pursuant to the provisions of a collective bargaining agreement, and as such may be filled by a qualified bargaining unit member, should one apply. Job openings: Number of openings: 2 Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3586?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Mechanical Engineering | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor - Mechanical Engineering Detroit, MI, United States | req3586 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/1/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor - Mechanical Engineering Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Assistant Professor - Mechanical Engineering at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): The Mechanical Engineering Department at Wayne State University invites candidates to apply for a tenure-track faculty position. We expect to make the appointment at the Assistant Professor level, but will consider senior-level candidates with a strong track record of major external funding and peer-reviewed publications. Duties involve teaching at both undergraduate and graduate levels, introducing new courses in the applicant’s respective field of expertise, developing a strong record of peer-reviewed journal publications, establishing an impactful and sustainable externally funded research program, supervising and mentoring both graduate and undergraduate students, and engaging in professional societies and university services. Qualifications: Applicants must hold a doctorate in mechanical engineering, aerospace engineering, or a closely related field, with expertise in one or more of the following areas: Artificial intelligence, autonomous systems, and connected mobility applied to the modeling, design, control, testing, and operation of mechanical and physical mobility systems. Advanced materials and manufacturing encompassing the implementation of artificial intelligence and machine learning in design and manufacturing, materials informatics and big data design, smart manufacturing, characterization and processing of materials, scalable and adaptable manufacturing processes. Vertical Take-Off and Landing (VTOL) technology, advanced propulsion technologies, including electric and hybrid systems, unmanned systems flight dynamics, control and autonomy, energy storage solutions to increase range and efficiency for electric VTOL aircraft. The anticipated starting date of employment is August 18, 2027. This position is a 9-month appointment. All applicants must submit their applications through the WSU Online Hiring System at https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home?c=waynetalent. Electronic applications must include a letter of application; curriculum vita; names, addresses, and contact information for at least three references, a personal statement regarding teaching and research activities (limited to 3 pages), and selected refereed journal publications (limited to 3 papers). Emailed applications will not be accepted. The review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and the search will remain open until the position is filled. Salary and rank of position will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. School/College/Division: H05 - College of Engineering Primary department: H0507 - Mechanical Engineering Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Academic/Faculty Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Working conditions: This position must be filled pursuant to the provisions of a collective bargaining agreement, and as such may be filled by a qualified bargaining unit member, should one apply. Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
