    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3700?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Hourly) - Department of Physiology",
      "description": "Research Assistant (Hourly) - Department of Physiology | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Research Assistant (Hourly) - Department of Physiology Detroit, MI, United States | req3700 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/16/2026 Back to Search Research Assistant (Hourly) - Department of Physiology Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Research Assistant (Hourly) - Department of Physiology at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): Perform laboratory based scientific techniques or clinical/population science (human subjects) research.  Design and perform experiments, recruit participants for studies by developing surveys and interviewing candidates.  Participate in implementing research objectives, ensuring compliance with all legal, regulatory and protocol guidelines.  Assist staff with experiments/research protocols as required. Communicate with principal investigator and Postdoctoral scholars/fellows on the status of research. Collect and log laboratory results, clinical outcomes and/or survey data. Evaluate and perform data analysis using graphs, charts or tables to highlight the key points of the research results collected in accordance with the research protocols as stipulated. P roofread and edit research data and documents to ensure accuracy. Assist principal investigator with writing methods and summarizing/organizing data  to be used in preparing and presenting presentations for professional, scientific and academic conferences and submittal to academic and scholarly peer-reviewed journals. Maintain the laboratory work areas including routine maintenance on laboratory equipment as required. Order and organize required research supplies and records.  Maintain cleanliness and organization of work station. Perform other related duties as assigned. Unique duties: Qualifications: Education: Bachelor's degree Bachelor’s degree in a related field of study, preferred. Requires advanced knowledge predominantly intellectual in character in a field of science or learning acquired by a prolonged course of specialized intellectual instruction. Experience: Entry level (less that a year of job-related experience) Experience performing basic research techniques or interacting with human subjects , preferred. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Ability to work independently. Ability to analyze, summarize and interpret research findings and data in reports and spreadsheets and communicate results. Knowledge of scientific integrity principles. Excellent time management and organization skills. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills and demonstrated ability to work with others in a collaborative environment. Microsoft office software skills. Preferred qualifications: School/College/Division: H06 - School of Medicine Primary department: H0606 - Physiology Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Research Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Hourly Wage Working conditions: Typical work environment may include a laboratory and/ or office environment.  May require working non-standard business hours including evenings and weekends.  May be required to travel to scientific conferences. Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3698?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Research Development Officer, Division of Research & Innovation",
      "description": "Research Development Officer, Division of Research & Innovation | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Research Development Officer, Division of Research & Innovation Detroit, MI, United States | req3698 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/15/2026 Back to Search Research Development Officer, Division of Research & Innovation Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Research Development Officer, Division of Research & Innovation at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Position Purpose: The Research Development (RD) Officer will be a member of the Research Development team in the Division of Research & Innovation (DORI) reporting to the Assistant Vice President for Research. The Office of Research Development (ORD) empowers Wayne State University faculty and researchers to pursue bold, competitive funding opportunities that advance discovery and impact. Through proposal development support, strategic partnership building, and faculty recognition initiatives, ORD provides the infrastructure and expertise researchers need to compete successfully for external funding, helping WSU strengthen its standing as an urban R1 research institution committed to excellence and community impact. Essential Functions : Project management on large-scale proposals: work with faculty and staff teams to develop well-organized, polished, competitive grant proposals that are responsive to funder requests. Related activities include, but are not limited to: · Providing grantsmanship guidance. · Establishing a timeline for items required for proposal submission. · Drafting templates for proposal and supporting documents. · Coordinating and collecting required materials from external partners and stakeholders. · Organizing and running red team reviews. · Facilitating connections to external partners and partnership management. Develop programming and resources for faculty and staff, such as toolkits, workshops, mentoring programs, etc. with the goal of helping position teams for success in pursuing external research funding. Work with AVP on forecasting and strategic planning for proactively responding to large-scale funding opportunities. Track federal funding opportunities and work with the Communications team to advertise those that are relevant to the WSU community. Work with WSU’s government relations firm on strategic opportunity discovery and matching. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Education : Bachelor’s degree Bachelor’s degree from an accredited college or university. Ph.D. or other advanced degree preferred. Experience : Expert (established subject matter expert, 7+ years’ experience). 7+ years' experience in Research Development, proposal development, and/or grant writing in higher education or master’s degree with 5+ years' experience in Research Development, proposal development, and/or grant writing in higher education. Experience in grant proposal development and scholarly writing and editing preferred. Demonstrated success working with teams to submit large-scale proposals (>$5 million) preferred. Training and/or experience in a variety of participatory facilitation practices (e.g., Strategic Doing, Liberating Structures, Ideas Labs, design sprints, or other facilitation methods and tools) that invite participants to share diverse perspectives/ideas and develop a shared vision preferred. Significant experience with conduct and/or management of sponsored research, e.g., familiarity with funders and/or funding mechanisms, peer review, federal merit review processes, etc. Program/project management experience with demonstrated ability to carry out projects to completion. Experience with administrative aspects of activity and event planning. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities : Knowledge of federal and non-federal research sponsors’ common requirements and anticipated funding goals. Ability to effectively provide training to others in the form of workshops and individual instruction. Strong initiative and demonstrated ability to work independently and in collaborative settings is essential. Must be able to work in a sensitive and confidential manner with proposals and research data, using independent judgment and professionalism. Commitment to strengthening broader impacts/societal impacts/community engagement and an understanding of the importance of these critical factors to the research community. Ability to collaboratively and professionally work with a wide variety of faculty, staff, and stakeholders with a range of personalities, opinions, perspectives, and approaches to the work. Preferred Qualifications: Ph.D. or other advanced degree preferred. School/College/Division: The Division of Research & Innovation Primary Department: H0905 - VP Research Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Staff/Administrative Funding/Salary Information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Annual Salary: To be determined Working Conditions: Work is primarily performed in an office environment. Job Openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3694?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Academic Services Officer III - Dept. Undergraduate Medical Education",
      "description": "Academic Services Officer III - Dept. Undergraduate Medical Education | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Academic Services Officer III - Dept. Undergraduate Medical Education Detroit, MI, United States | req3694 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/15/2026 Back to Search Academic Services Officer III - Dept. Undergraduate Medical Education Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Academic Services Officer III - Dept. Undergraduate Medical Education at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): This position is an integral part of Medical Education/UME as it directs the faculty development for new and existing faculty, residents responsible for teaching the clinical clerkship courses for the M3 medical students. Essential Duties: Essential Functions (Job Duties) Build sustained, trust-based partnerships with pre-clerkship faculty and course directors. Serve as a primary point of contact for teaching, assessment, and facilitation support. Provide individualized coaching that supports teaching effectiveness, professional growth, and adaptive problem-solving in dynamic instructional settings. Collaborate with faculty to refine session design and instructional strategies. Support active learning approaches and small-group pedagogy. Help faculty design instruction that is feasible, flexible, and responsive to changing curricular needs. Promote alignment across courses and longitudinal curricular threads. Support faculty in developing and refining feedback practices. Partner with faculty to review learner performance data and identify practical improvement opportunities. Support alignment with competency-based and programmatic assessment expectations. Collaborate with course directors to review course evaluations and curriculum data. Translate evaluation findings into actionable, faculty-centered improvements. Support continuous quality improvement and accreditation efforts. Support inclusive, psychologically safe learning environments for students and faculty. Encourage thoughtful experimentation and innovation in teaching and assessment. Support effective use of educational technologies and responsible AI tools. Coordinate with clerkship-phase faculty development to support vertical integration. Participate in the regional and national activities of the AAMC for faculty development. Perform other duties or projects assigned. Unique duties: Collaborate with the administration to create and execute a professional development protocol that is essential to the development of new and existing faculty and staff responsible for teaching and supporting the clinical clerkship component of the MD curriculum.\nServe in a mentoring capacity to faculty during instructional planning by supporting them with the integration of instructional best practices that are rooted in the science of learning.\nDevelop meaningful and intentional relationships across the University to create\nlearning resources that meet the needs of the School of Medicine and the Office of\nMedical Education and its stakeholders.\nTrack and report both quantitative and qualitative results from training activities through course evaluations and in-person observation to identify trends and opportunities for faculty development trainings and coaching.\nDirects the UME Coaching Program for faculty. Qualifications: Master’s or doctoral degree in education, learning sciences, instructional design, or a health professions doctorate with educational expertise. Experience supporting or teaching in pre-clerkship or early clinical medical education. Demonstrated ability to build trust, coach faculty, and work effectively in complex organizational environments. Work effectively with diverse academic, cultural and ethnic backgrounds of faculty, staff and students Highly effective oral and written communication skills Preferred qualifications: Experience with competency-based or programmatic assessment approaches. \nExperience coaching faculty, facilitating small groups, or using active learning strategies. \nEngagement in educational scholarship or innovation. School/College/Division: H06 - School of Medicine Primary department: H0671 Employment type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Academic/Faculty Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Hourly rate: Salary minimum: Salary hire maximum: $90,411.00 Working conditions: This position is an AAUP-represented position. Job openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3693?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor, Child and Family Development",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor, Child and Family Development | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Tenure-Track Assistant Professor, Child and Family Development Detroit, MI, United States | req3693 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Tenure-Track Assistant Professor, Child and Family Development Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Tenure-Track Assistant Professor, Child and Family Development at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Position Purpose: The Merrill Palmer Skillman Institute (MPSI) for Child and Family Development at Wayne State University (WSU) is excited to welcome a new colleague to our collaborative and interdisciplinary research community. We invite applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning August 2027. The successful candidate will hold a joint appointment between MPSI and an academic department aligned with the candidate’s disciplinary background. We welcome applications from scientists whose work addresses the development, health, and well-being of infants, children, adolescents, or families. Applicants’ research programs should align with the urban mission and values of WSU and MPSI and contribute to research, training, and community engagement that advance knowledge and solutions benefiting children, families, and communities. Areas of particular interest include research on the processes and contexts that shape the development and health of children and families from infancy through young adulthood; risk, resilience, prevention, and intervention; and translational research that can drive innovations that promote health and well-being, including emerging technologies and new models of healthcare. We are also interested in work that examines influences across multiple levels of context, from individuals and families to communities and populations; however, we are open to work that addresses other important questions in developmental science and family health. Essential Functions : Successful candidates will have a strong, independent program of research, evidence of or clear potential for external funding, and demonstrated quantitative expertise in developmental research methods. Responsibilities at MPSI include research, professional service, training and mentorship, and community engagement, along with teaching responsibilities in the academic department in which the candidate holds a joint appointment. About Wayne State: Wayne State University is a premier, public, urban research university located in Detroit, Michigan with a bold Strategic Plan . The 200-acre traditional and high-tech campus is in the heart of Midtown, offering unparalleled access to cultural experiences, community involvement, and corporate career opportunities. Wayne State University is the third largest institution of higher learning in Michigan and the most diverse campus in the state with over 24,000 students from every U.S. state and nearly 80 countries. Wayne State is home to 13 colleges offering 375 degree programs, as well as seven interdisciplinary research centers and institutes. wayne.ed ￼ About Merrill Palmer Skillman Institute for Child and Family Development: The Merrill Palmer Skillman Institute (MPSI) is a vibrant interdisciplinary research institute within Wayne State University’s Division of Research and Innovation (DORI; research.wayne.edu ). With support from research cores and facilities across campus, current research at MPSI examines how developmental processes, during gestation through young adulthood, shape trajectories of mental and physical health, learning, and well-being within individuals, families, communities, and society. Through community-engaged work, we strive to translate developmental science into innovative programs, policy advancements that prioritize children and families, and workforce training models that help children, families, and communities thrive . Along with other research institutes and centers within DORI, we address pressing societal issues through innovation and research excellence. The Merrill Palmer Skillman Institute (MPSI), founded in 1920, has been at the forefront of developmental science that translates research excellence into practice and policies that strengthen child and family well-being. With a distinguished history of impactful scholarship and practice in child and family development, including opening one of the nation’s first laboratory nursery schools and pioneering early studies on nutrition and high-quality education models, MPSI has worked to advance the development, health, and well-being of infants, children, adolescents, young adults, and their families. Today, the Institute brings together 11 core and 12 affiliate faculty, 16 predoctoral trainees, external funding from federal, state, and philanthropic sources, and programs that reach thousands of professionals and community members each year. Building on more than a decade of significant growth and accomplishment, MPSI is seeking its next f.aculty member to contribute to the Institute’s continued research excellence and community impact. To learn more about MPSI, please visit our website at https://mpsi.wayne.edu , the 2023-2027 strategic plan at: https://mpsi.wayne.edu/strategic-plan , and our most recent Biennial Report at: https://mpsi.wayne.edu/about/2023-2024-mpsi-report.pdf . Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. or equivalent in a field related to child and family development before the start date. Strong record of research productivity and high potential for significant external funding. Strong evidence of a novel research agenda and demonstrated potential for excellence in research, community engagement, service, and teaching. Demonstrated commitment to effective mentoring, teaching, research practices, and community engagement across varied populations, communities, and contexts, along with a strong record of collegiality and collaborative work. Demonstrated committed to interdisciplinary research and team science. Application Procedure : Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026 , and continue until the position is filled. To apply go to https://jobs.wayne.edu job. Click on “Wayne Talent career site” and search for posting “ Req # 3693. ” Applications should submit the following directly to the online system: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Key reprints/preprints Statement of research interests Statement of teaching interest and evidence of teaching effectiveness, if available Statement that addresses how your research fits with the missions of MPSI, DORI, and WSU List of at least three references who could be contacted Inquiries can be directed to Dr. Julie Wargo Aikins, Chair of the Search Committee, at julie.wargo.aikins@wayne.edu School/College/Division: The Division of Research & Innovation Primary Department: H0910 - Merrill Palmer Institute Employment Type: Regular Employee Job type: Full Time Job category: Academic/Faculty Funding/Salary Information: Compensation type: Annual Salary Annual Salary: To be determined Working Conditions: Normal Faculty Environment Job Openings: Number of openings: 1 Reposted position: No Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
    },
    "https://waynetalent.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/2/home/requisition/3690?c=waynetalent": {
      "title": "Temporary Clerical, Undergraduate Admissions",
      "description": "Temporary Clerical, Undergraduate Admissions | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Temporary Clerical, Undergraduate Admissions Detroit, MI, United States | req3690 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Temporary Clerical, Undergraduate Admissions Wayne State University is searching for an experienced Temporary Clerical, Undergraduate Admissions at its Detroit campus location. Wayne State is a premier, public, urban research university located in the heart of Detroit, Michigan where students from all backgrounds are offered a rich, high-quality education. Our deep-rooted commitment to excellence, collaboration, integrity, diversity and inclusion creates exceptional educational opportunities which prepare students for success in a global society. Essential functions (job duties): Job\nPurpose Provide temporary records processing support\nfor Undergraduate Admissions Operations. Process undergraduate applications,\ntranscripts, documents, and student records across multiple University systems\nin accordance with established procedures. Review and maintain records for\naccuracy and completeness and respond to routine application-related inquiries. Essential\nFunctions (Essential\nfunctions are the primary duties/major job responsibilities that an employee\nmust be able to perform, with or without reasonable accommodation. The\nessential functions are listed in order of importance.) Process undergraduate applications, transcripts, and supporting documents across multiple University systems. Review, upload, index, and maintain documents and update application records, statuses, checklist items, and other information according to established procedures. Review applications and student records for completeness and accuracy. Perform data entry and routine record cleanup; identify and resolve missing, duplicate, or inconsistent information within established guidelines. Identify and consolidate duplicate records (prospect, application, school, relationship, etc.), including record merging and associated document and data maintenance. Review and maintain school/organization information, including school record cleanup and the creation and maintenance of CEEB/school codes. Respond to routine internal and external inquiries regarding application status, receipt of documents, and outstanding requirements. Log communications, processing activities, and record updates as appropriate. Process assigned records and work queues accurately and timely while maintaining confidentiality of student information. Refer exceptions and non-routine matters to appropriate staff. Perform\nother related duties as assigned. Qualifications: Education High school completion (or GED) Experience Experienced (minimum 2 years of job-related experience) Previous clerical or administrative experience preferred Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Accuracy and attention to detail when working with high volumes of records and data. Basic computer and data-entry skills with the ability to learn multiple University systems. Ability to follow established procedures, organize and prioritize assigned work, and communicate effectively with internal and external constituents. Previous records processing, data entry, clerical, admissions, or student services experience preferred. School/College/Division: H92 - VP Student Affairs Primary department: H9242 - Admissions (H9242) Employment type: Temporary Employee Job type: Fractional Time Job category: Staff/Administrative Funding/salary information: Compensation type: Hourly Wage Hourly rate: TBD Salary minimum: Salary hire maximum: TBD Working conditions: Office environment Job openings: Number of openings: 2 Reposted position: No Reposted reason: None (New Requisition) Prior posting/requisition number: Background check requirements: University policy requires certain persons who are offered employment to undergo a background check, including a criminal history check, before starting work. If you are offered employment, the university will inform you if a background check is required. Apply Now Share Save Job Wayne State University provides equal employment opportunities to all employees and applicants for employment without regard to race, color, sex, national origin, religion, age, sexual orientation, familial status, marital status, height, weight, disability, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.  The university welcomes applications from persons with disabilities and veterans.  Wayne State is an equal opportunity employer. Equal Opportunity Employer including persons with disabilities and veterans Wayne State University Human Resources * 5700 Cass Avenue * Suite 3638 AAB Detroit, MI * 48202"
